meta {
  name: Relay
  seq: 2
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{relayApiToken}}
}

docs {
  # Relay

  The MediaMTX API the app polls for who is live, plus the one media leg that is a plain HTTP GET.

  The API is the single source of truth for which streams exist and who is reading them.
  It exposes counters and no rates, so the per-path bitrate the app shows is a `bytesReceived` delta between two fetches at a steady cadence (`backend/internal/relay`).

  A fetch is the path list and then one per-protocol list per reader type present, because the path list names a reader by type and id and says nothing else about it.
  The lists are relay-wide, so two paths served over SRT are two slices of one answer.

  A list whose listener is switched off answers 404, which is a fact about the relay's configuration and not about its health.

  The TLS variants answer the same shape on the same base: `rtspsconns`, `rtspssessions` and `rtmpsconns`.
  The app folds them into the same transport, which listener a reader arrived on being a fact about the relay rather than about the leg.

  Every relay this repository runs reads one configuration and authenticates every connection, a relay on this machine and a deployment alike (`deploy/mediamtx-groups.yml`).
  The API binds loopback in both, so this folder is `Local`'s.

  It is granted to nothing a group token carries, so reading it takes an operator's own credential.
  `groupd` prints one off the signing key `task relay` draws:

  ```bash
  bin/groupd -api-token 2h -key dev-relay/signing-key.pem
  ```

  The token grants the API for the window given and goes in `relayApiToken`, without the scheme word, the header above carrying that.
}
